<!--Post: 6829--><!--Time: 1108026604-->
<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#cccccc"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST6829"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Thursday, February 10, 2005 - 1:10 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->I think I&#39;ve got the doubled letter thing.  They&#39;re genotypes.  Like GG, gg, and &#40;especially&#41; Gg, as in &#34;FO Gg y&#34;.  Could be any letter - G is just the prototypical one.  He said &#34;way off bases&#34; &#40;as in nucleic acid bases - DNA&#41; when he was getting tired of us not getting it.  If you follow that line of reasoning, RIAC is out there in google-land - Italian Register of Chromosome Anomalies &#40;Registro Italiano delle Anomalie Cromosomiche - R.I.A.C.&#41;.  Could just be a poser, needling us on the chimera thing, or maybe BTK is saying the swabathon hasn&#39;t even come close to him yet.  This sort of stuff, including DNA fingerprinting, is now undergrad biochem, so it&#39;s not unbelievable that BTK would know it.  If Tom really thinks this guy is BTK, then it may prove that he&#39;s no dummy on DNA tech.  Or it could just be &#34;Prince Albert in a can of agarose.&#34;<!--/Text--></td></tr>
