<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST7124"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><a href="mailto:bmodule_244@hotmail.com" target="_blank"><!--/email--><!--name-->Bmodule<!--/name--></b></a>
</a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Bmodule<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->2-2005<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Saturday, February 12, 2005 - 3:25 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->And speaking of shooting wide...yall are gonna love this: <BR> <BR>With a wichita street map in hand, I looked up some street names names. I found west street of course, then texas street, couldn&#39;t find a susan street but I found susie street. They are both roughly on Maize road which runs north/south just west of the airport &#40;ITC&#41;. Susie is in Schulte &#40;crossroad Macarthur road&#41; and texas is north &#40;crossroad maple street&#41; <BR> <BR>If you go too far north you find Toh-N-Hah trail. For the sake of argument we will assume it is named after an Indian chief. If you go east to a bit past Oatville &#40;past West Street&#41; you find Custer Streets to both the north and south of you. We&#39;ll call him a Chief too. So if you go to far north or east you hit chiefs, so you are off bases. <BR> <BR>Now, we know BTK is becoming a country boy so here we are in the sticks, and judging by the creeks, hilly and fairly easily secluded sticks. <BR> <BR>If you look on 47th south just west of Haysville you have a fairly substancial grave yard &#40;remember Bored to death, no bones about it, smell of lillies, grave joke, under your nose, etc&#41;and better still, on Kellog &#40;crossroad Millbrook&#41;you find Hillcrest cemetary. <BR> <BR>Notice each cemetary has a nearby railroad. <BR> <BR>And to tie things up, one must educate thy self&#40;which I read means attend a religious[thy] school [educate]&#41;. So across kellogg from the airport we have Saint Francis of Assissi school, which I believe is a university ,but I couldn&#39;t find a thing on the web. But I have visually witnessed a very impressive looking school there, and I&#39;m sure it is affilliated with a church. <BR> <BR>So my premise is that BTK will be active in the south/west rural outskirts of Wichita in the near future. <BR> <BR>But wait...there is more! <BR> <BR>If you were to drive Southwest Boulevard &#40;42&#41; and keep going through Schulte,and keep driving for about 40 min to an hour, guess where you would end up? That is correct. Anthony Kansas. So if the new post about the guy who has had run-ins with BTK is bonified, That part of Wichita, or at least the rural areas, would be the area he is most familiar with. So that fits in well also. I almost forgot, there are roosters in the rural areas that crow.  <BR> <BR>So  I guess that pretty much wraps up the whole case. Glad I could be of service. <BR> <BR>P.S. I don&#39;t know if the guy posting was BTK or not, but I sure felt less anxious when he was talking...at least for a little while it felt like we knew what he was up to. <BR> <BR><!--/Text--></td></tr>
