<!--p:--><tr bgcolor="#f7f7f7"><td valign=top width=200><a name="POST9401"><font face="Verdana" size="1"><b><!--email--><!--/email--><!--name-->Pax56<!--/name--></b></a><br>Username: <!--field:uname-->Pax56<!--/field--><br><br>Registered: <!--field:registered-->12-2004<!--/field--><br><!--field:ip_address--><!--/field--></font></font></td><td valign=top><font face="Verdana" size="1"><table width=100% border=0 cellspacing=0 cellpadding=0><tr><td align=left><font face="Verdana" size="1">Posted on Sunday, February 27, 2005 - 12:33 am: &nbsp;&nbsp;</font></td><td align=right></td></tr></table><hr noshade size=0><!--Text-->Very interesting possibilities, John.  I wonder, too.  I made a couple of pleas myself, though not in that thread - so did Pastor Dave &#40;dpinva&#41;.  BTK is so atypical - the possibility that he has some kind of fragmented or proto-conscience may actually be there.  There are many of us who would have loved to see him turn himself in, or at least keep up the [strategically foolish] communications with the police and media, on the possibility that he&#39;s theologically redeemable.  I would like to think that his communications were a subconscious wish to be caught, although the idea that he simply wanted attention is a stronger contender, IMHO, until proven otherwise. <BR> <BR>If BTK were having an internal dialog on these web pages, it would be strange, but this case has never been lacking for strangeness.<!--/Text--></td></tr>
